WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:01.868
-Joy, thank you so much

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:02.868
for coming on the show.

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:03.869
It's great to have you on.

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:04.871
-It is great to be on.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.405
Jimmy, it's so fun being
on the show.

00:00:06.405 --> 00:00:07.507
I'm so excited.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.409
I apologize for being
in my closet...

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.877
-[ Laughs ]
-...but we're having
some work done

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:13.279
in the house this week

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.047
-I love it!
-and I wanted to make sure

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:17.717
it was quiet, so it's the
quietest place in the house.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.252
So I'm in my closet.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.620
-Oh, this is fantastic.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.889
Any fan of shoes,
people are like,

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.524
this is like they're
geeking out right now.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:27.160
They're so jealous
of your collection.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.896
I have so many things
I wanna talk to you about.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.564
Congrats on "The ReidOut,"
by the way.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:34.433
It launched in July
and it's doing fantastic,

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:35.635
crushing in the ratings.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.169
I was looking at some
information about you

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:38.804
and you're the first
woman of color

00:00:38.804 --> 00:00:41.840
to anchor a primetime news
show on MSNBC

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.610
and you're also the only
Black woman currently anchoring

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.047
a primetime news show
on any of the major networks.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.215
-Yeah.
-That is --

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.817
What an accomplishment.

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.086
What does that mean to you?

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:54.320
-Yeah, it's daunting, right?

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.323
I mean, and when it
kinda sunk in

00:00:57.323 --> 00:00:59.859
that I was gonna be doing this,
it felt really big.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.127
You know, I grew up, you know,

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:03.662
being a news junkie
from sixth grade on.

00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:06.432
And, you know, everyone that was
sort of telling me the world

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:09.102
and telling me about the news,
the Ted Koppels of the world,

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:10.870
the Dan Rathers,
all the people that I admired,

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.505
it was all white guys, right?
-Yeah.

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:15.007
-There were very few
people like me

00:01:15.007 --> 00:01:16.409
that I could really see on TV,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.111
so when I finally saw
Gwen Ifill, it was like...

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:20.313
♪ Ahhh ♪
...your know?

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:22.648
I just revered her
'cause it was so exciting.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:25.184
Carole Simpson and Gwen Ifill
were the only Black women

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:27.153
that I can remember growing up.

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:28.955
-Yeah.
-Connie Chung, the only
other woman of color.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:32.391
So the very few women of color
that I've seen in this space,

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:34.193
I've always gravitated
toward them.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:36.062
I finally did get a chance
to meet Gwen Ifill

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.330
before she passed.
I was so excited.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.999
I ran across the street
in Selma, Alabama,

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:41.267
and threw myself on top of her

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.268
to give her a hug.
-Wow!

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.902
-She was very nice about it

00:01:43.902 --> 00:01:46.072
and let me take a selfie
with her, which I treasure, so,

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.707
yeah, it's big because,
for some little kid, you know,

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:52.045
some little Black girl,
I'm like that to them, in a way.

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:54.080
Like they're maybe seeing me
and feeling like,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.615
"Okay, I can do this.
This is something

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.149
that I could do."
-Yeah. That's exactly right!

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:58.150
-Yeah.
"I can do that.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.752
I can be that," yeah.

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.988
We should talk about some
of the news that's happening.

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:05.858
Obviously,
today is the 19th anniversary

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:07.960
of the September 11th attacks.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.330
-Yeah.
-Were you working in news then?

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.964
What do you remember
about that day?

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:14.199
-I was. I mean, by then,
we had three young kids,

00:02:14.199 --> 00:02:16.636
but we were living in Florida
and I was now working

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:20.006
for the NBC affiliate
WTVJ in Miami

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.774
and I remember it was
my day off, actually.

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.243
9/11, I was having an off day

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:25.778
and my brother
had come up from Denver

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.013
and was staying with us
and I remember him yelling,

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.249
"Oh, S-word!

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:31.851
A plane just hit
the World Trade Center."

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.687
And, at first, I thought maybe
it was just a wrong-way plane

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.255
'cause we had actually
had a story like that

00:02:36.255 --> 00:02:37.589
not long before that, in Miami,

00:02:37.589 --> 00:02:39.792
where a guy just went off-track
and hit a building.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.561
So I'm thinking this is
just a wrong-way plane.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.363
By the time I walked
down the stairs,

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:47.300
the second plane hit
and that's when we all realized

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.035
this is not a wrong-way plane.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:50.569
This is serious.

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:54.240
Phone went off.
I had to go in to work.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.510
It was shocking.
I think I was so stunned

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.078
for the first,
probably, you know,

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:01.580
six or seven hours,
that it didn't hit me,

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.383
but, when it did, I will admit,
I'm not ashamed to admit

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:06.786
I just cried
and I don't cry at work.

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:08.154
I try not to ever cry at work,
you know,

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:09.622
no matter how tough the story.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:12.692
But I cried that day
and then, the day that --

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:16.295
I don't know if you remember
the Boys Choir of Harlem

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:18.597
did a thing where they went
into Yankee Stadium

00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:21.034
and they sang
"We Shall Overcome,"

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:22.802
to try to cheer New York up

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:24.804
-Ohh!
-and I just -- I lost it.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.539
-Gosh!
-Like, you know, yeah.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:30.443
-And then, to see the city
come back and also --

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:31.810
is pretty inspiring as well.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.045
-Yeah.
-And to see the first responders

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:35.080
and everyone helping,
doing whatever they can

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.916
to help each other
and I was like, "Oof!"

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:39.385
-New York is still
the greatest city in the world.

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.386
-Oh!
-It just is.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.155
It's the most resilient.

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:43.689
People are like,
"New Yorkers aren't friendly."

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:46.058
No, New Yorkers will give you
the shirt off their back.

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:47.894
They will do anything for you.

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:50.262
New York comes together
like no other city

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:53.366
and rebounds like no other city,
even with what's happening now.

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.334
-I agree.
-I was just in New York
recently.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:56.735
New York is fightin'
its way back.

00:03:56.735 --> 00:03:58.738
Nothing can stop New York.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.139
-What else is happening
in the news?

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:04.043
I guess everyone's still talking
about Bob Woodward's book

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:05.944
and those tapes!

00:04:05.944 --> 00:04:07.513
-Ah!
-I just don't quite

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:09.182
understand it.
What are your takes?

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:10.850
So, basically,
the president admitted

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.017
that he downplayed the threats

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:15.254
-Yeah.
-of coronavirus.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:19.725
It just had a lot of stuff,
but what was he thinking?

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:21.460
Why would he do that?

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:22.962
-And here's the thing, Jimmy.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:26.465
It's not even as if Bob Woodward

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.000
called the White House
switchboard and said,

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.702
"Let me talk to Trump.
Let me talk to him just

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:33.505
for five minutes."
No, Trump called him.

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:37.076
On one of the critical phone
calls where he admitted

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:40.546
that he was just downplaying it,
even though he knew

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:44.550
that this was a deadly virus,
that -- oh! -- is airborne.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.720
Not only that, the day
that he called Bob Woodward

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.355
and gave him that
on-the-record interview

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.623
where he's like,
"I'm playing it down.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.625
I like to play it down,"
he held a rally that day.

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:56.228
I mean, he literally,
in the contemporaneous period.

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:58.297
And then, kept holding rallies.

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:00.466
And then stopped for a while
and then went right back

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.235
and did it, in Tulsa,
the day after Juneteenth.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:04.837
This man has known
the whole time.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:07.306
You know, I did a commentary
on the show the other day,

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:09.809
where I said, "Look,
at the end of the day,

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:12.145
Donald Trump
has lost his credibility

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.313
with people like me,"
people who cover him

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.783
day in and day out
and who have --

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:18.618
you know, I've written
a book about him.

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:21.954
I've interviewed all
of his biographers, nearly,

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:23.289
people who've worked with him.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:25.358
people who worked
on "The Apprentice."

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.660
Very difficult for me to take
him at face value when he talks.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.363
But his supporters do.

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:32.198
They believe everything he says.

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:34.500
It's them
he's primarily lying to.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.002
It's them who he's convinced
not to wear masks.

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:40.539
It's them who will take
his advice and try bleach

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:42.007
and thinking that
that will help them.

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:43.308
It's them!
-Oh, my God.

00:05:43.308 --> 00:05:45.411
-And then, they put
the rest of us in danger

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:47.413
because they won't
protect themselves.

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.549
-I'm sure you saw
the rally last night,

00:05:49.549 --> 00:05:51.451
with everyone with --
It was just --
-Unbelievable.

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:53.486
-I-I --
-And they played "Fortunate Son"

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:55.020
as like the intro song.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.556
-It's just so -- it's bizarre.

00:05:57.556 --> 00:06:00.660
I wanna talk
about your big project

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.596
with Peacock and MSNBC
tomorrow night at 10:00,

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.365
-Yeah.
-but it's a documentary
called "The Sit-In."

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:09.568
I'm hosting "The Tonight Show,"
so I know the story,

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:11.737
but a lot of people
might not know this story.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:13.873
Do you wanna explain
what "The Sit-In" is about?

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.174
-Yeah. Jimmy,
I didn't know it, either.

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:17.043
Like I did not know that --

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:18.344
And, you know,
we talked early on

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:21.380
about the idea of, you know,
me hosting, you know,

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.549
a primetime news show.

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:26.819
As you know,
late night is very similar,

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:28.954
in terms of the demographics
of the people

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:32.457
who've dominated late night,
you know, since the 1950s.

00:06:32.457 --> 00:06:34.093
You know, Johnny Carson,
of course,

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:37.063
being the guy who made
late night into late night,

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:39.164
-Yeah.
-created this
incredible platform

00:06:39.164 --> 00:06:41.299
that you, you know,
are blessed to continue

00:06:41.299 --> 00:06:43.369
and that we're thankful
that you're continuing, so.

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:45.204
But you also know
that the platform

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:48.741
has generally been
about a breath, right?

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:52.110
It's like Walter Cronkite
at 6:00 and then, a deep breath

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.879
-Yeah!
-for late night,
when you can just chill.

00:06:53.879 --> 00:06:55.981
-Yeah.
-Right?
And that's what it was.

00:06:55.981 --> 00:06:57.783
But Johnny had an ethos.

00:06:57.783 --> 00:06:59.785
Johnny, he was a little,
you know,

00:06:59.785 --> 00:07:01.753
he was a little bit of a social
gangster, a little bit.

00:07:01.753 --> 00:07:03.122
He was like, "I'm a little
gangster with this.

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.024
I'm gonna do something big."

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:09.595
He took this platform and he
gave it to the first Black man

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:10.829
to ever host in late night,

00:07:10.829 --> 00:07:12.731
and that Black man is
Harry Belafonte.

00:07:12.731 --> 00:07:16.702
February 1968, middle part
of the civil rights movement.

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:19.371
You know, violence and riots
had been going on

00:07:19.371 --> 00:07:21.607
over police killings
and abuse of Black people.

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:23.509
It was a really difficult
time in America.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:26.812
You know, Johnny Carson
hands this week over

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:28.981
to Harry Belafonte and he says,
"Do whatever you want with it."

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:30.816
And Harry says, "You know
what I'm gonna do with it?

00:07:30.816 --> 00:07:31.884
I'm gonna go big.

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.118
Yes, I'm gonna have on
Aretha Franklin,

00:07:33.118 --> 00:07:35.788
Buffy Sainte-Marie,
like great entertainers.

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:38.791
But I'm also gonna have on
my political friends,"

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:41.594
political people,
and he had on the two biggest,

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:43.595
in terms of social gospel --

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:44.763
Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.

00:07:44.763 --> 00:07:46.465
-I've seen it.
-and Robert F. Kennedy.

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:48.367
And he does the last --

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.635
the interviews,
especially with Dr. King,

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:51.336
I think this was
his last recorded interview.

00:07:51.336 --> 00:07:53.004
-It's a great documentary.

00:07:53.004 --> 00:07:54.039
It's called "The Sit-In."

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:55.208
It's on Peacock currently

00:07:55.208 --> 00:07:56.875
and tomorrow night on MSNBC.

00:07:56.875 --> 00:07:58.177
-Yes.
-I wanna show a clip.

00:07:58.177 --> 00:07:59.912
Here is a clip
from "The Sit-In."

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:01.213
Check it out.

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:03.248
-Well, I'm delighted
to be here, Harry,

00:08:03.248 --> 00:08:04.283
and I'll tell you one

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:07.352
of the reasons
I'm so happy to be here.

00:08:07.352 --> 00:08:09.455
I flew out of Washington
this afternoon

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:13.893
and, as soon as we started out,
they notified us

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:17.029
that the plane
had mechanical difficulties

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:19.565
and I don't want to give you
the impression that,

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:23.835
as a Baptist preacher, I don't
have faith in God in the air.

00:08:23.835 --> 00:08:25.237
It's simply that I've had

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:27.172
more experience with Him
on the ground.

00:08:27.172 --> 00:08:29.641
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:29.641 --> 00:08:32.578
-I was kind of amused that he
knew how to get laughter,

00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:35.414
which is something that you
don't see in his speeches

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:37.249
because he's so serious.

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:40.085
My thing was like, "Oh!

00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:41.320
He can tell a good joke."

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.155
-Fantastic!
Joy Reid, everybody.

00:08:43.155 --> 00:08:45.157
I also wanna mention
"The Man Who Sold America,"

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:47.459
your best-selling book
is now in paperback,

00:08:47.459 --> 00:08:49.361
with brand-new chapters.
Go check that out.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:52.565
Also, please watch
"The Sit-In" on Peacock now.

00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:53.765
We'll be right back
with a performance

00:08:53.765 --> 00:08:55.067
from the Flaming Lips.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:56.869
Thank you again, Joy,
for being here.

00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:58.905
-Thank you, Jimmy,
for having me. Bye-bye.

